  "narrative": "Scientific synthesis: no candidate survives the clinical-significance and uncertainty standard used throughout this run.\n\nI retained the same outcome-specific benchmark as earlier: because dependency-score means are negative, a clinically meaningful difference requires a 10% absolute-mean benchmark in the hypothesized direction. For outcome_014 the benchmark is about 0.0093; for outcome_008 it is about 0.0282. Positive signed estimates are consistent with the claim direction, usually stronger dependency when the exposure contrast makes the outcome more negative. Acceptance requires more than a point estimate above the benchmark: the relevant interval lower bound must reach the clinical benchmark, and for validated claims I used the high-confidence validation interval.\n\nH114 is rejected and closed. The original outcome_014 feature_063 level_002 versus level_001 discovery estimate was +0.0361, but its 95% lower bound was only +0.0022, below the 0.0093 benchmark. Independent validation is now available and is in the opposite direction: estimate -0.0190 with interval [-0.0698, +0.0317]. Although the validation interval is wide and still contains clinically positive values, the direct test does not support a clinically meaningful positive association, and the claim cannot be maintained.\n\nH255 is rejected and closed. The discovery feature_055 level_004 versus level_003 contrast on outcome_008 had a signed estimate of +0.0360, above the outcome_008 benchmark, but its 95% lower bound was only +0.0065, far below 0.0282. The independent validation result is small and inconclusive-to-null: signed estimate +0.0046, high-confidence interval [-0.0367, +0.0459]. The lower bound is far below the clinical benchmark, and the validation point estimate is not near the discovery effect. This is a failure of the registered independent check.\n\nBecause H255 was the key validation candidate for the remaining feature_055 category signal, and because prior coherence evidence failed, I also close the feature_055 outcome_008 line as not supported. H248 and H253 remain discovery-only zero-excluding effects with lower bounds near zero, and the related validation contrast H255 failed; cross-outcome coherence also failed earlier, with H251 and H252 in the wrong direction for their clinical claims and H250 not clinically interval-supported. Thus I reject H248 and H253 as evidence-supported clinically meaningful associations in this dataset. This is a negative dataset-specific assessment, not proof that no effect exists or cannot exist in another population.\n\nSupported findings: the accepted set remains empty. No measured predictor or subgroup association in this supplied dataset has a validated or sufficiently interval-supported clinically meaningful effect under the 10% benchmark.\n\nNegative and nonreplicated findings: feature_042 main/subgroup contrasts, feature_032, feature_063, feature_055, and many other binary or categorical comparisons show estimates that are either near null, interval-imprecise, not clinically interval-supported, or discordant between discovery and validation. The pattern is most consistent with multiple-comparison-prone borderline discoveries rather than a robust predictor effect.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty: some intervals remain too wide to exclude all clinically meaningful effects, and many predictors or level combinations remain untested or underpowered. Causal interpretation and external generalizability are not established.\n\nNext analysis: I do not register new post hoc comparisons because there is no active candidate with a coherent, interval-supported signal. The most informative future work would be a pre-specified, low-multiplicity validation strategy in new data, or a tightly scoped confirmatory screen with interval-level clinical acceptance before further discovery. Further untargeted mining of this dataset is more likely to increase false-positive burden than to produce a credible association.",
